"See how a twist (a torque) turns the axis of a spinning wheel instead of tipping it over, measure how the turning depends on the spin and on the lever arm, and use the result to understand why Earth’s axis slowly circles the sky once every {#} years.",
"<strong>Spin and angular momentum.</strong> A spinning wheel has angular momentum, a kind of spin “amount” that points along its axle. The faster it spins, the more angular momentum it has.",
"<strong>Torque.</strong> The wheel’s weight pulls down on it at a distance d from the post. A force applied at a distance from a pivot makes a twist called a torque. The farther out the force acts, the bigger the torque. A wheel that is not spinning simply tips over and falls.",
"<strong>Precession.</strong> A torque on a spinning wheel does not tip it over. It changes the direction of the angular momentum a little at a time, sideways, so the axle swings slowly around the post. A bigger torque turns the axle faster; more spin makes it turn more slowly, because there is more angular momentum to turn. For this demo the time for one turn around the post is proportional to the spin rate and inversely proportional to d.",
"<strong>Earth is a spinning top.</strong> Earth spins once a day, and its spin makes it bulge at the equator: its diameter through the equator is {#} km larger than through the poles. Earth’s axis is tilted {#}° from upright compared with its orbit. The Sun and the Moon pull on the bulge and try to twist Earth’s axis upright. Because Earth spins, the axis does not stand up; it precesses instead, sweeping out a cone in space. One full turn takes about {#} years ({#} years at today’s rate of {#} arcseconds per year, from the IAU {#} precession model).",
"<strong>The pole star.</strong> Today Earth’s north pole points within {#}° of Polaris, so Polaris barely moves in our sky. As the axis precesses, the pole moves slowly around a circle in the sky. About {#} years ago (around {#} BC) the pole was near the star Thuban in Draco, and in about {#} years it will be near the bright star Vega.",
